Excellent question! You're right to be concerned about scaling to multi-gigabyte files. Let me show you how to optimize for massive datasets with **streaming-friendly approaches** and **memory-efficient tree construction**.

## Key Optimizations for Large-Scale Huffman Coding

### 1. **Streaming Frequency Counting (No Full String in Memory)**

Instead of loading the entire file into a string, we process it in chunks:

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstring>

class StreamingHuffmanCoder {
private:
    static constexpr size_t CHUNK_SIZE = 64 * 1024 * 1024; // 64MB chunks

    // Count frequencies from a file stream (no full file in memory)
    std::unordered_map<char, uint64_t> countFrequenciesStreaming(const std::string& filename) {
        std::unordered_map<char, uint64_t> freqMap;
        std::ifstream file(filename, std::ios::binary);

        if (!file) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Cannot open file: " + filename);
        }

        std::vector<char> buffer(CHUNK_SIZE);
        while (file.read(buffer.data(), CHUNK_SIZE) || file.gcount() > 0) {
            size_t bytesRead = file.gcount();
            for (size_t i = 0; i < bytesRead; ++i) {
                freqMap[static_cast<unsigned char>(buffer[i])]++;
            }
        }
        return freqMap;
    }
};
```

